.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-volume-write 1  "tapectl-volume-write " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-volume\-write \- Write staged data to volume
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl volume write\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-loaded\fR] [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-init\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-move\-to\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-missing\-escrow\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR] [\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR] [\fB\-\-full\-confirm\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\-staging\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fILABEL\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Write staged data to volume
.PP
Refuses any volume not left `initialized` by `volume init` — a sealed, retired, erased or quarantined volume is not a write target (ADR\-0012); no flag overrides it.
.PP
Once the volume is sealed and confirmed, staging is released if every unit written has reached its resolved `min_copies` (ADR\-0012, 2026\-10\-07 item 2: after a quick confirm too); otherwise it is kept and the output names each unit that is still short. A release that fails is a warning and the exit status stays 0. `\-\-keep\-staging` never releases; `staging clean` does it later.
.PP
A further copy in one command (ADR\-0012 2026\-10\-15 item 59): `tapectl volume write L6\-0003 \-\-init \-\-full\-confirm \-\-move\-to offsite` initialises the loaded cartridge, writes the volume, reads every file back, and moves the sealed volume (and its cartridge) to `offsite`. Each step keeps its own checks and the command stops at the first one that fails: an init that refuses writes nothing, and nothing moves unless the write sealed and confirmed.
.PP
A unit that allows spanning (`allow_spanning`, ADR\-0012 item 82) may have its slices on more than one tape. The write lays out first the slices still owed of a span in progress, then whole units, then units that may span, last; the drive\*(Aqs early warning, not the plan, ends such a unit on the tape (items 84, 122 and 123), which needs the drive\*(Aqs `post_warning_bytes`. The write names each unit the tape holds as a range of slices, the whole units it leaves for the next tape (still staged), and, once a range completes a copy across tapes, the tapes to keep together (item 134). A span continues only onto a cartridge of the generation it began on (item 86).
.PP
Exit status, shared by every command that writes a tape: 0 = sealed and confirmed. 2 = an error with the medium untouched: nothing was written (or a usage error). 3 = the confirm was inconclusive: the tape is sealed, `volume resume` re\-enters the confirm. 4 = interrupted part\-way: `volume resume` continues. 5 = aborted (end of tape, a slice that changed): write the label again. 6 = the medium was proven bad and the volume quarantined. 75 = the catalog was busy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-loaded\fR
Write the loaded tape without naming it: File 0 is read (a `volume identify` contact) and the tape is written only when it is one of this catalog\*(Aqs volumes, `initialized` and with nothing written to it; the volume chosen is printed. Any other state, or a tape this catalog does not know, is refused naming it. The write that follows takes its usual File 0 consent check on its own handle. Cannot be combined with a label or `\-\-init`
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path). Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes. Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
See `volume init \-\-force` — same override, same limits. With `\-\-init` it goes to the init only: the write that follows is never forced, so a cartridge swapped in between the two is refused at the write\*(Aqs own File 0 check
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-init\fR
Initialise the loaded cartridge as this volume first (`volume init <label>`: the same function, the same File 0 consent check and refusals, ADR\-0003 and ADR\-0012 item 57), then write it with the tape still loaded. An init that refuses writes nothing and exits 2. Only `\-\-force` is taken from `volume init`\*(Aqs flags; when a cartridge needs `\-\-generation` or `\-\-cartridge`, run `volume init` yourself first. ADR\-0012 2026\-10\-15 item 59
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr. Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/
.TP
\fB\-\-move\-to\fR \fI<LOCATION>\fR
Move the volume, with its cartridge, to this location once it is sealed and confirmed (`volume move <label> \-\-to LOCATION`). The location is checked before anything is written. Nothing moves unless the write sealed and confirmed. A move that then fails is a warning naming the `volume move` command that finishes it; the copy stands and the exit status stays 0
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-missing\-escrow\fR
Seal slices that were staged before the escrow recipient existed (ADR\-0005). Only reachable for tapes copied forward via `read\-slices`/`compact\-read`, since `stage create` refuses to stage without escrow; use it to migrate a dying pre\-escrow cartridge, knowing the copy stays unrecoverable by the escrow key
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME.
.TP
\fB\-\-prewrite\-hash\fR
Full\-hash every staged slice from disk before the tape moves (tri\-layer L1). Off by default (ADR\-0012, 2026\-09\-30): the write then checks each slice exists at its recorded size, and a slice that rotted in staging is caught while streaming (clean abort, tape left unsealed). This costs one extra full read of the batch
.TP
\fB\-\-fill\-ceiling\fR \fI<FILL_CEILING>\fR
Fill this volume to at most this fraction of its capacity, for this write only: `0.99` or `99%`. Overrides the drive\*(Aqs `fill_ceiling` (default 97%, ADR\-0012). The pre\-write check refuses a layout above the ceiling and names by how much
.TP
\fB\-\-full\-confirm\fR
After sealing, read every file back and hash it against the front index (tri\-layer L3, hours on a full cartridge). Off by default (ADR\-0012, 2026\-10\-06): the confirm then reads the front index and the seal marker, and a passing one seals the volume with no full readback recorded — `audit` and `report verify\-status` name it until `volume verify` runs one
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\-staging\fR
Keep the staged slices after the write seals and confirms, even when every unit written has reached its resolved `min_copies`. Without this flag the staging is released then; release it later with `tapectl staging clean`
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fILABEL\fR]
Volume label. A write names its volume: the label ties the data to the volume `volume init` consented to (ADR\-0003). Give it, or give `\-\-loaded` instead
